= The Fall of Marx IV

Following the controversial civil service reforms of 1927, strongly opposed by Stegerwald and the trade union leadership, the reignition of class antagonisms has proven to be detrimental to internal unity. As such, the national leadership has scrambled to find a unifying policy agreeable to the divergent wings of the party. In the end, the need for a comprehensive Reich School Law has proven to be the answer to reunite the party through shared religious concerns and Kulterpolitik, which will be significantly easier due to the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP’s**</span> presence in government.  

Based on the provisions of Article 174 of the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> Constitution, the education system in Germany has been generally left to its pre-war status in the Landers. While it was a necessary compromise between the conflicting educational policies of the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> coalition partners, this has however led to a noticeable lack of denominational schools in Catholic-dominated states like Baden and Hesse. 

With the aid of Walter von Keudell from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, we have managed to draft a school law enshrining the primacy of interdenominational schools in Germany. While this would satisfy large portions of our base, the consent of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, rooted in anti-clerical tradition, might prove to be a challenge.

- @reject: The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> expresses their outrage
- @accept: Stresemann rallies his party to seek a compromise

